  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on comparing the least probable outcomes of each lottery and prefers the one with the more favorable (higher) such outcome. In this case, Lottery B has a least probable outcome of 5000 Euros, which is more favorable than Lottery A’s least probable outcome of 5 Euros. The individual does not explicitly mention focusing on the least probable outcome per se, but they do break the tie between lotteries with equal expected values by choosing the one with the chance of a large sum (5000 Euros), which corresponds to the rare but favorable outcome. While the individual does not articulate the rule in terms of 'least probable outcome,' their reasoning aligns with giving decisive weight to the presence of a rare high gain. The focus on the 'chance on the große summe' despite equal expectations suggests attention to the most favorable low-probability event. Therefore, the individual’s deliberation is consistent with the essence of the decision reason, even if not framed probabilistically in the same terms. The information considered (presence of a high-value rare outcome) and the justification (choosing based on that outcome when expectations are equal) align closely with the reason’s logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 85
}

